# Ownership for this repo. A context file without a named owner rots inside a quarter,
# and a rotted context file is worse than none — so this file is the other half of the
# "owned by name" line in AGENTS.md.
#
# CODEOWNERS only has teeth once the repository is configured to give it teeth. Three
# settings, all under Settings -> Branches -> branch protection rule for `main`:
#   1. Require a pull request before merging
#   2. Require review from Code Owners
#   3. Require status checks to pass — add `tier1` ONLY. Never add the tier-2 job.
# Those need repo admin. Until they are set, this file documents intent and blocks nothing.
#
# The copy-me version with placeholders is templates/CODEOWNERS.example.

*                       @limsijie93
/AGENTS.md              @limsijie93
/templates/             @limsijie93
/.github/               @limsijie93
/example/               @limsijie93

# The control plane, named separately from the `*` rule above, and not because `*` failed to
# cover it. `*` covers it for review; what `*` does not do is SAY that this is the file the
# tier-0 guards are wired through, so a reviewer skimming a diff has nothing telling them the
# stakes are different here.
#
# tools/guard_protected_paths.py already refuses AGENT edits to both of these, on the argument
# that a guard the agent can disable is not a guard. This is the other half of the same
# argument, one level up: a guard the agent cannot disable, and a human can delete in a pull
# request nobody looked closely at, is also not a guard. Catch-all review is not guard
# self-protection.
#
# It changes nothing mechanically today — the owner is the same person, and CODEOWNERS has no
# teeth until branch protection is configured (three settings, above). It is here so that when
# there is more than one owner, the split is already drawn at the place it matters.
/.claude/               @limsijie93
/tools/guard_*.py       @limsijie93
